# ---- rclone credentials: NEVER commit these ----
rclone.conf
*.conf
# *.conf is deliberately broad. To commit an example config anyway:
#   git add -f rclone.conf.example
.rclone/
rclone-*.json

# Google OAuth client files downloaded from Cloud Console
client_secret*.json
*credentials*.json
*token*.json

# ---- private key material: NEVER commit these ----
# The *.key and *.pem rules further down do NOT match an extensionless SSH key
# such as id_rsa — which is exactly how private keys end up committed by an
# unfiltered `git add .` in a directory that also holds dotfiles. Hence these.
.ssh/
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*_rsa
*_ed25519
*.ppk

# GPG private key material. Deliberately NOT a blanket *.asc rule:
# rclone-release-key.asc (repo root) is a PUBLIC key and is committed on purpose.
.gnupg/
secring.*
*.gpg
*private*.asc

# ---- rclone install artifacts, if you ever unpack one inside the repo ----
/rclone
rclone-v*/
rclone-v*.zip
rclone-current-*.zip
SHA256SUMS

# ---- environment / secrets ----
.env
.env.*
secrets/
*.key
*.pem

# ---- this toolkit's own generated marker ----
# Written by `42sync onboard-apply` at an onboarded repo's root (see
# ADR-0001/0003 in design/, and write_marker() in bin/42sync) -- a
# regenerable record of that repo's own remotes, never hand-edited. This
# repo is itself a valid onboarding target (confirmed for real: it already
# had its own GitHub remote, so onboarding just wrote the marker, no new
# repo needed) -- ignored here so `git status` stays clean for anyone using
# this toolkit on its own repo, not just other people's projects.
.42sync-remote

# ---- logs and temp ----
*.log
*.tmp
bisync-state/

# ---- OS and editor junk ----
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/
.claude/settings.local.json
